## Refactored with Async/Await

Here's the refactored version with several improvements:

```javascript
async function getUser(id) {
  const rows = await db.query('SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = ?', [id]);

  if (!rows.length) {
    throw new Error(`User with id "${id}" not found`);
  }

  return rows[0];
}
```

### Usage Example

```javascript
// Basic usage
try {
  const user = await getUser(1);
  console.log(user);
} catch (err) {
  console.error('Failed to get user:', err.message);
}

// Or with .catch()
const user = await getUser(1).catch(err => {
  console.error('Failed to get user:', err.message);
});
```

---

### What Changed and Why

| Before | After | Reason |
|---|---|---|
| `callback(err)` | `throw new Error(...)` | Async functions reject the returned Promise on throw |
| `callback(null, rows[0])` | `return rows[0]` | Async functions resolve the returned Promise on return |
| Manual error threading | `try/catch` at call site | Cleaner, built-in error propagation |

---

### Important Caveat

This assumes **`db.query` already returns a Promise**. If it's still callback-based, you'll need to promisify it first:

```javascript
// Option 1 — Node's built-in util.promisify (simplest)
const { promisify } = require('util');
const dbQuery = promisify(db.query.bind(db));

async function getUser(id) {
  const rows = await dbQuery('SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = ?', [id]);

  if (!rows.length) {
    throw new Error(`User with id "${id}" not found`);
  }

  return rows[0];
}

// Option 2 — Manual Promise wrapper (most control)
function dbQuery(sql, params) {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    db.query(sql, params, (err, rows) => {
      if (err) reject(err);
      else resolve(rows);
    });
  });
}
```

> **Tip:** Most modern database libraries (pg, mysql2, better-sqlite3) support Promises natively — check your driver's docs before manually promisifying.
